ult is the following.\n\nTheorem 1.1\n\nLet $\\mathrm{\\Omega }\\subset {\\mathbb{R}}^{n}$ be a bounded domain with $n\\ge 2$, and let $u\\in {C}^{4}(\\mathrm{\\Omega })$ be a convex solution of (1). Assume there exist a function $w\\in {C}^{2}(\\mathrm{\\Omega })$ satisfying $w>u$ in Ω and $w=u$ on ∂Ω, and a constant $K>0$ such that${u}^{ij}{w}_{ij}\\ge -K\\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}\\mathit{\\text{in}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}\\mathrm{\\Omega },$ where $[{u}^{ij}]$ is the inverse matrix of ${D}^{2}u$. Then for every $x\\in \\mathrm{\\Omega }$,${(w(x)-u(x))}^{({2}^{n}+1)(2n+1)}|{D}^{2}u(x)|\\le C(n,K,{\\Vert u\\Vert }_{{C}^{0,1}(\\mathrm{\\Omega })},{\\Vert w\\Vert }_{{C}^{0,1}(\\mathrm{\\Omega })}).$\n\nThe proof is based on a Jacobi inequality for function $b=\\mathrm{det}(I+{D}^{2}u)$, as observed in [20]. Combining this inequality with a cutoff function φ, we establish what we call a boundary Jacobi for φb. Applying an integral approach to this boundary Jacobi inequality, we iteratively reduce the terms from $\\int {(\\phi b)}^{p}{\\sigma }_{k}$ to $\\int {(\\phi b)}^{p}{\\sigma }_{k-1}$. This establishes an induction formula with respect to $p=1,2,\\cdots $, and thus obtain a Pogorelov type {W}^{2,n+1}${W}^{2,n+1}$ estimate, which states that φb is bounded in {L}^{n+1}${L}^{n+1}$. Finally, following the spirit of Moser's iteration, we prove that the {L}^{\\infty }${L}^{\\infty }$ norm of ${\\phi }^{2n+1}b$ is controlled by {L}^{n+1}${L}^{n+1}$ norm of φb, thereby completing the proof of the theorem.\n\nTheorem 1.1 implies the following estimates directly.\n\nCorollary 1.2 Pogorelov-type estimates and interior estimates\n\n  * (i)\n\nIf $u=0$ on ∂Ω, then taking $w\\equiv 0$ (so that (2) holds trivially) gives${(-u(x))}^{C(n)}|{D}^{2}u(x)|\\le C(n,{\\Vert u\\Vert }_{{C}^{0,1}(\\mathrm{\\Omega })}),$ with $C(n)=({2}^{n}+1)(2n+1)$ and $x\\in \\mathrm{\\Omega }$.\n\n  * (ii)\n\nIf the bounded convex domain Ω is normalized, i.e., ${B}_{r}(0)\\subset \\mathrm{\\Omega }\\subset {B}_{R}(0)$ for some $0<r<R$, then for any $ϵ>0$ and ${\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{ϵ}=\\{x\\in \\mathrm{\\Omega }|\\mathrm{dist}(x,\\partial \\mathrm{\\Omega })>ϵ\\}$, we have${\\Vert {D}^{2}u\\Vert }_{{L}^{\\infty }({\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{ϵ})}\\le C(n,ϵ,r,R,{\\Vert u\\Vert }_{{C}^{0,1}(\\mathrm{\\Omega })}).$\n\nAs a standard consequence of the interior Hessian estimates, we obtain the interior smoothness of strictly convex solutions. See [2], [7] and references therein.\n\nCorollary 1.3\n\nLet u be a strictly convex solution of (1) on a bounded domain $\\mathrm{\\Omega }\\subset {\\mathbb{R}}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 2$. Then u is smooth in Ω.\n\nHere and below, $C(\\cdots )$ denotes a positive constant depending only on the listed quantities, which may change line by line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuantitative Stability and Numerical Resolution of the Moment Measure Problem (https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.09914)\nciteturn6academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Title: Quantitative Stability and Numerical Resolution of the Moment Measure ProblemAuthors: Guillaume Bonnet, Yanir A.Rubinstein\nTitle: Quantitative Stability and Numerical Resolution of the Moment Measure Problem\nAuthors: Guillaume Bonnet, Yanir A. Rubinstein\nDate: Fri Apr 10 21:17:20 2026\n\nThe moment measure problem consists in finding a convex function $ψ$ whose moment measure, i.e., the pushforward by $\\nabla ψ$ of the measure with density $e^{-ψ(\\,\\cdot\\,)}$, is prescribed. It is highly non-linear and less understood than the related optimal transport problem. We establish a quantitative stability estimate for this problem. This estimate validates, as well as leads us to introduce, an approach to the numerical resolution of the moment measure problem inspired by semi-discrete optimal transport, consisting in approximating the prescribed measure by a finitely supported one. We describe a Newton m